{
 "topicId": "mt_o2vTJv1KoM",
 "title": "Acceleration",
 "audience": "learner",
 "keyIdea": "Acceleration is how quickly velocity changes: a = change in velocity ÷ time, measured in m/s².",
 "explain": {
  "kid": [
   "Speeding up is called acceleration. A bike that goes 2 faster each second has an acceleration of 2.",
   "Slowing down is a minus acceleration. We call it slowing down."
  ],
  "adult": [
   "a = (v − u) ÷ t, where u is the starting velocity and v is the final one. A car going from 0 to 20 m/s in 5 s has a = 4 m/s². Each second it gains 4 m/s.",
   "If v is less than u, a is negative: the object is slowing down. On a velocity–time graph, acceleration is the gradient."
  ]
 },
 "familyTip": "In a car or on a bike, notice the speed every few seconds while speeding up. Work out how much faster each second.",
